# Node.js Security Guidelines

Runtime and HTTP-server security for Node.js. Australian English spelling throughout.

---

## Child Process Safety

- Use `execFile`/`spawn` with an argument array — never `exec` or `shell: true` with interpolated input
- Validate executables and arguments against an allow-list
- Set a `timeout` and `maxBuffer` on every child process

---

## File System and Paths

- Resolve user-supplied paths with `path.resolve(base, input)` and verify the result starts with `base + path.sep`
- Reject null bytes; never pass user input to `require()`/`import()`
- Avoid `eval`, `new Function`, and `vm` for untrusted code — `vm` is not a sandbox

---

## SSRF Prevention

- Validate user-supplied URLs against an allow-list of hostnames and schemes (`https:` only)
- Resolve DNS and block loopback, link-local (`169.254.0.0/16`, cloud metadata), and RFC 1918 ranges
- Limit redirects and re-validate each redirect target

---

## HTTP Hardening

- Apply `helmet()` (Express) or `@fastify/helmet`; configure CSP explicitly for HTML responses
- Limit body size (`express.json({ limit: '100kb' })`, Fastify `bodyLimit`)
- Set server timeouts (`headersTimeout`, `requestTimeout`) to resist slow-loris attacks
- Enforce HTTPS and HSTS in production; set `trust proxy` correctly behind a load balancer
- Rate-limit public endpoints (stricter on auth); back with Redis when running multiple instances; return `429` with `Retry-After`

---

## Secrets

- Load secrets from a secrets manager (AWS Secrets Manager, Vault, Azure Key Vault) in production; `.env` files for local development only
- Configure Pino `redact` for authorisation headers, cookies, and password/token fields
- Run with `NODE_ENV=production` so frameworks hide error details

---

## Dependency Security

- `npm ci` in CI; `npm audit --audit-level=high` as a blocking step
- Renovate or Dependabot for updates; review install scripts of new dependencies (`npm install --ignore-scripts` for untrusted packages)

---

## Checklist

- [ ] No shell interpolation; child processes have timeouts
- [ ] User-supplied paths and URLs validated (traversal, SSRF)
- [ ] `helmet`, body limits, and server timeouts configured
- [ ] Rate limiting on public and auth endpoints
- [ ] Secrets from a manager; redacted in logs
- [ ] `npm audit --audit-level=high` passes in CI
